# Part Three — The Examiner's Record of Topic 3

## Every question, 2016–2026

@py topic_questions 3

## What the record reveals

- **Nine full questions and one short note in eleven papers** — a steady, mid-weight topic (==9.5 visits==), and **22 of the 140 MCQs**.
- **Two families carry it.** The **waves** (2019, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025) and the **movement in Pakistan** (2016, 2022, 2024, 2026, plus APWA/WAF in 2018 and NGO-isation in 2026).
- **An exact repeat.** 2019 Q3 and 2023 Q5 are word-for-word the same. The examiner is willing to repeat — learn the waves-and-Pakistan answer thoroughly.
- **The link is always wanted.** Four of the five waves questions ask about Pakistan or women's lives.
- **Every Pakistan question has a twist:** strengths and weaknesses (2016), past versus emerging trends (2024), legal rights to bodily autonomy (2026), NGO-isation and the WAD critique (2026).
- **The questions are getting sharper.** 2026 quoted scholarly phrases — "locus of resistance", "technocratic project", "depoliticized".
- **The UN has never been a full question**, though MCQs on Mexico 1975, Beijing 1995 and CEDAW 1996 recur. It is due.

## Five likely questions

1. "Critically evaluate the contribution of the four UN World Conferences on Women to the global women's movement. What has Beijing+30 revealed?" *(Chapter 6.)*
2. "Is there a fourth wave of feminism? Discuss its features and its expression in Pakistan." *(Chapters 4 and 9.)*
3. "The Aurat March has divided Pakistani society. Discuss its demands, its critics and its significance." *(Chapters 8, 9 and Part Two.)*
4. "Discuss the role of the Women's Action Forum in the struggle against discriminatory laws in the 1980s." *(Chapter 8.)*
5. "How far has the women's movement in Pakistan succeeded in translating activism into law and law into change?" *(Chapters 8 and 11.)*

These are not solved here; the Prediction Papers include answers to predicted questions. Plan each as an answer map for practice.
